{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="content" %}
# Content design

## The video follows a logical flow that directly supports its learning objectives

Organise the video around explicit intended learning outcomes. Each section, explanation, example and visual should contribute to what learners are expected to know or perform. Remove content that is interesting but unnecessary. This applies the coherence principle: learners generally perform better when extraneous information is excluded.[^mayer]

Use a structure appropriate to the subject rather than enforcing a rigid template. A useful default may follow [BOPPPS][boppps-model] or [CARD][card-model] model and consist of a brief orientation, clearly identified sections, explicit transitions and a final synthesis or application prompt. 

Break complex explanations into meaningful segments and allow learners to process one segment before moving to the next. A meta-analysis found small-to-moderate positive effects of learner-paced segmentation on retention and transfer.[^segmenting] Guidance for educational-video production similarly recommends keeping videos focused on their learning goals and using signals to highlight important concepts.[^video-guidelines]

[^mayer]: [Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing in Multimedia Learning (Chapter 14) - The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning")

[^segmenting]: [Meta-analysis of the segmenting effect](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-018-9456-4 "Rey, G. D., Beege, M., Nebel, S., Wirzberger, M., Schmitt, T. H., & Schneider, S. (2019). A Meta-analysis of the Segmenting Effect. Educational Psychology Review, 31, 389–419.")

[^video-guidelines]: [Effective educational videos](https://doi.org/10.1187/cbe.16-03-0125 "Brame, C. J. (2016). Effective Educational Videos: Principles and Guidelines for Maximizing Student Learning from Video Content. CBE—Life Sciences Education, 15(4), es6.")


## The duration of the video is concise and reasonable (6 min sweetspot)

Shorter videos boost engagement and learning. A large-scale MOOC study found median viewing times ≈6 minutes, regardless of longer actual length[^mooc], and another study showed students watching short (~8 min) videos scored 9% higher than those watching ~55 min lectures.

Brief videos match attention spans better (studies indicate drop-offs after 6-9 min) and encourage active re-engagement. Quality Matters and other experts recommend keeping lessons under ~6-10 min. Short clips can be easily rewatched, supplemented with activities and reused in other learniung paths.

[^mooc]: [An empirical study of MOOC videos](https://doi.org/10.1145/2556325.2566239 "How video production affects student engagement: An empirical study of MOOC videos. Philip J. Guo, Juho Kim, Rob Rubin. School of Computing Massachusetts Institute of Technology, University of Rochester & EdX")

## Branded intro/outro is not longer than 3 seconds

Keep any intros/outros extremely short. Use minimal animations or a logo flash of 1-2 seconds if needed. Very short intros avoid losing attention. Viewers generally "want to jump right into content". 

Minimising non-instructional sequences eliminates unnecessary delays and prevents early drop-off. Quick branding (e.g. a flash of a logo) can provide professionalism without boredom. Especially when your course contain 30+ videos with exactly the same intro & outro in each.

A brilliant example of the extremely short but still speaking out intro in instructional video is the Google Career Certification courses on Coursera.[^google-courses]

[^google-courses]: [Google Career Certification Course Example](https://www.coursera.org/learn/foundations-data/lecture/aA1Wv/welcome-to-the-google-data-analytics-certificate "Foundations: Data, Data, Everywhere. A part of Google Data Analytics Professional Certificate. Coursera")

## A brief text introduction accompanies the video and addresses the intended learners

Briefly explain the video’s purpose, relevance, intended audience and any required prior knowledge. Use language and examples appropriate to the learners, and avoid repeating the video’s opening narration.[^pretraining]

[^pretraining]: [The pre-training principle](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139547369.017 "Mayer, R. E., & Pilegard, C. (2014). Principles for Managing Essential Processing in Multimedia Learning: Segmenting, Pre-training, and Modality Principles. In The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 2nd ed. Cambridge University Press.")


## Color contrast is checked and accessible for texts and visuals

Ensure all on-screen text and visuals have high contrast and are easily distinguishable. For example, use dark text on a light background with a contrast ratio at least 4.5:1. 

Sufficient contrast makes text and graphics readable for low-vision and colour-blind learners, and under various lighting. It is an [accessibility][accessibility] requirement to accommodate ~8% of men (and 0.5% of women) with colour-vision deficiency. High contrast also reduces eye strain for all users.[^wcag]


 [^wcag]: [Web Content Accessibility Guidelines](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/ "Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1. W3C Recommendation. 06 May 2025")

## Audio is clear and free from distracting noise

Ensure speech and essential sounds are intelligible, with consistent volume and no clipping, excessive reverberation or background noise that masks the content. Use music and contextual sounds only when they serve a clear instructional purpose.[^audio-quality]

[^audio-quality]: [How audio quality influences perceptions](https://doi.org/10.1177/1075547018759345 "Newman, E. J., & Schwarz, N. (2018). Good Sound, Good Research: How Audio Quality Influences Perceptions of the Research and Researcher. Science Communication, 40(2), 246–257.")


## Video quality is stable and visual dynamics support learning

Ensure that all learning-relevant details remain clear and legible, without distracting camera movement, blur, lighting changes or compression artefacts. Use changes in shot, pace, animation and visual focus to demonstrate processes, signal structure or direct attention—not merely to make the video appear more dynamic.

Avoid both prolonged static presentation and excessive editing. The appropriate pace and amount of movement depend on the subject, learners and complexity of the material; production polish should never compete with the explanation or introduce unnecessary cognitive load.


## Accurate closed captions or subtitles are available

Provide synchronized, selectable closed captions for all speech and meaningful sounds. Review automatically generated captions for accuracy, timing, speaker identification, terminology, numbers and non-speech information. Provide translated subtitles as separate language tracks where needed.[^captions],[^caption-benefits]

Where useful, add an interactive transcript that learners can search and use to navigate to specific points in the video. A transcript complements but does not replace synchronized captions.

[^captions]: [Captions and subtitles](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/captions/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Captions/Subtitles. Web Accessibility Initiative.")

[^caption-benefits]: [Video captions benefit everyone](https://doi.org/10.1177/2372732215602130 "Gernsbacher, M. A. (2015). Video Captions Benefit Everyone. Policy Insights from the Behavioral and Brain Sciences, 2(1), 195–202.")


## Essential visual content is explicitly described in the narration

Verbally identify and describe visual information needed to understand the video, including actions, diagrams, data, interface changes and relevant on-screen text. Use precise references instead of phrases such as “as you can see” or “over here,” so the explanation remains understandable without relying entirely on sight.[^visual-description]

Describe only information that contributes to meaning. Where essential visual content cannot be incorporated naturally into the narration, provide a separate audio description or descriptive transcript.

[^visual-description]: [Description of visual information](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/description/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Description of Visual Information. Web Accessibility Initiative.")


## Key outcomes and takeaways accompany the video

Provide a concise list of what the video covers and what learners should understand or be able to do. Keep it visible alongside the video so learners can assess its relevance before watching and quickly revisit the essential points afterwards.[^video-summaries]

Include only the central outcomes and ideas. Do not use the summary as a substitute for watching when demonstrations, explanations or context are essential. Support materials should extend access, review or application—not simply duplicate the video. Transcripts improve accessibility and searchability, while active-learning materials can strengthen comprehension and transfer.

[^video-summaries]: [Writing and reading summaries of educational videos](https://doi.org/10.1145/3170358.3170382 "van der Zee, T., Davis, D., Saab, N., Giesbers, B., Ginn, J., van der Sluis, F., Paas, F., & Admiraal, W. (2018). Evaluating Retrieval Practice in a MOOC: How Writing and Reading Summaries of Videos Affects Student Learning. Proceedings of the 8th International Conference on Learning Analytics and Knowledge, 216–225.")


## Relevant support materials provided with the video

Provide materials that help learners access, review or apply the content, such as a searchable transcript, accessible copies of essential visuals, slides, references, key terminology, worksheets or practical examples.

Where appropriate, include a focused retrieval, reflection or application activity. Keep support materials concise, current and clearly connected to the video’s intended learning outcomes.[^active-learning]

[^active-learning]: [Active learning strategies in video learning](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.edurev.2025.100708 "Zhang, Y., Li, R., Pi, Z., & Yang, J. (2025). Active Learning Strategies in Video Learning: A Meta-analysis. Educational Research Review, 48, 100708.")


## Localized versions are available for target regions

Provide localized content where language or regional context could limit understanding or relevance. Depending on learners’ needs, this may include translated subtitles or transcripts, additional audio tracks, or fully localized video versions.[^multilingual-education] [^captioned-video]

Adapt terminology, examples, units, cultural references and on-screen text where necessary—not only the spoken words. Human-review translations, label each language and locale clearly, and keep all localized versions synchronized when the source video changes.[^captions]

[^multilingual-education]: [Global guidance on multilingual education](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/languages-matter-global-guidance-multilingual-education "UNESCO. (2025). Languages Matter: Global Guidance on Multilingual Education.")

[^captioned-video]: [Captioned video for second-language learning](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2013.07.013 "Montero Perez, M., Van Den Noortgate, W., & Desmet, P. (2013). Captioned Video for L2 Listening and Vocabulary Learning: A Meta-analysis. System, 41(3), 720–739.")

{% /pattern-list %}

